TEXT: In a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) transformer layer, the routing mechanism is designed to activate only a sparse subset of parameters for each input token, significantly enhancing computational efficiency. When a token enters an MoE layer, it first passes through a lightweight gating network, typically a small linear projection followed by a softmax function. This gating network calculates a probability score for every available expert, indicating how relevant each expert is to the specific token’s features.

Instead of processing the token through all experts, the system selects only the top-k experts with the highest scores. For instance, if there are 64 experts but k=2, only two experts process each token. The token’s representation is then multiplied by these gating scores and passed to the selected experts for specialized processing. Finally, the outputs from the active experts are aggregated and combined with the residual stream. This sparse activation means that while the model may have billions of parameters, the active computation per token involves only a tiny fraction of them.

This sparsity makes offloading experts to CPU RAM highly practical. In dense transformer models, all parameters must reside in high-bandwidth GPU VRAM simultaneously, creating a severe memory bottleneck. In contrast, MoE models allow for a "memory-compute" trade-off. Since only a few experts are active at any given time, the majority of experts can remain dormant in the slower but larger CPU RAM. During inference, only the necessary expert weights are fetched from CPU RAM to the GPU
