TEXT: In a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) transformer layer, the routing mechanism is designed to activate only a sparse subset of parameters for each input token, significantly enhancing computational efficiency. When a token enters an MoE layer, it first passes through a lightweight gating network, typically a small linear projection followed by a softmax function. This gating network calculates a probability score for every available expert, indicating how relevant each expert is to the specific token’s features.

Instead of processing the token through all experts, the system selects only the top-k experts with the highest scores. The token’s representation is then multiplied by these gating scores and sent exclusively to the chosen experts for processing. The outputs from these active experts are aggregated and combined to form the final output for that layer. This sparse activation means that while the model may have billions of parameters in total, only a tiny fraction is computed for any single forward pass.

This architecture makes offloading experts to CPU RAM highly practical due to the decoupling of computation and memory. Since only a few experts are active at any given time, the GPU does not need to hold all expert weights in its high-speed VRAM simultaneously. Instead, the majority of experts can reside in the larger, slower CPU RAM. During inference, only the weights for the top-k selected experts are transferred from CPU RAM to GPU VRAM on-demand. Because the number of active experts is small, the data transfer volume is minimal, and the latency incurred by PCIe transfers is
